
            PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);

            // an assertion using assert.that @@@ new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x is equal to @@@ new_arrList_IndexOf_x

            PexAssert.AreEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);

            // an assertion using assert.that @@@ arrList_contains_x is true

            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```

# <a name="visual-studio-code-test-explorer-test-tab"></a>[Visual Studio Code Test Explorer (Test Tab)](#tab/visual-studio-code-test-explorer)

Visual Studio Code doesn't provide built-in support for automatic generation of fuzz tests and PEx tests. However, you can write your own custom fuzz tests and PEx tests.

# <a name="command-line-nunit-console"></a>[Command Line (NUnit Console)](#tab/command-line-nunit-console)

In order to perform fuzz or mutation tests, it is required to have a method which provides random input data. Mutation testing tools such as Mutation Analysis Tool (MAT) from Pitest support mutation testing.

Here's an example structure of a fuzz testing method using NUnit framework:

```csharp
[Test]
[Category("Fuzz")]
public void Fuzz_Test_Method_Name([Values(int1, int2, int3, ..., intn)] int input)
{
    // Test code goes here using input
}
```

In this example, NUnit framework's `[Values]` attribute is used to supply a set of test values.

You can create a NUnit fuzz test by creating a new test method and assigning it the "Fuzz" category. This method can be passed a wide range of inputs.

Pitest is similar to Mutation Analysis Tool, a mutation testing library available in Java language. However, NUnit doesn't support mutation testing out of the box. If you want mutation testing, you will need to use a different framework such as JUnit.

```csharp
[Test]
[Category("Mutation")]
public void Mutation_Test_Method_Name([ValueSource("GetMutationTestData")] int input)
{
    // Mutation testing code goes here using input.
}

public static IEnumerable<int> GetMutationTestData()
{
    // Return your mutation test data.
    yield return int1;
    yield return int2;
    //...
}
```

In this example, the `[ValueSource]` attribute provides data from a method to the test method. This is useful for mutation testing where mutants are compared to the original source code. 

Remember to install the NUnit console runner using the NuGet package manager in order to use NUnit. Also, NUnit supports test categorization to enhance test management. You can categorize your tests as explained above.

```powershell
# Install NUnit Console Runner via NuGet
Install-Package NUnit3TestProject.coveragexml
```

To run the tests, use the NUnit console runner:

```powershell
# Run all the tests
dotnet test

# Run all the tests in a specific category
dotnet test --filter FullyQualifiedName~Tests.Category.Fuzz
```
-----

# <a name="summary"></a>Summary

Unit testing is a vital part of software development that ensures that each portion of code works correctly. In addition, fuzz testing and mutation testing are essential tools in ensuring your software is robust and can handle edge cases. It's always important to have unit tests, though they may not cover all edge cases, to avoid unexpected failures during the testing or production. The tools and techniques we reviewed here are crucial for a robust testing approach.

