A: 
The Pex tool analyzes the input values (both properties and fields) of a method. For Pex to be able to find the valid test inputs for a parameter, it must be possible for the tool to create any valid input that the method can accept-that is, any legal value, a minimum value, and null values, etc.


A: The Pex tool analyzes the input values (both properties and fields) of a method. For Pex to be able to find the valid test inputs for a parameter, it must be possible for the tool to create any valid input that the method can accept - that is, any legal value, a minimum value, maximum value, null values, and more.

For instance, if a method accepts an integer parameter and you are testing it, the tool will try input values like 0 (minimum value of an integer), 1 (somewhere in the range), max int value(maximum value of an integer), and even null.

However, it is worth noting that, depending on the nature of your methods and classes, certain types of input might be considered invalid or might generate unexpected results - for example, if your method requires a string parameter that cannot be null, Pex will definitely try to pass a null value as a test input.

One strategy to deal with this situation is to mark the parameter as [PexAssumeNotNull]. This instructs Pex to ignore the parameter during testing - so if the method expects a non-null value, Pex won't pass a null one as a test input.

Additionally, make sure to always take a look at the Pex explorer window when you run your unit tests - this will display potential problems with your test inputs and can give you clues about possible edge cases to consider.

In--------------------		Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);

	    // @@@ index of x in old array list is more than or same as -1
        TestHelpers.AssertIndexOf(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListContainsX, Old_index);

	    // @@@ index of x in new array list is equal to specified index 
	    TestHelpers.AssertIndexOf(New_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListContainsX, index);

	    // @@@ last index of x in old array list is more than or same as -1 
        TestHelpers.AssertLastIndexOf(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListContainsX, Old_index);

	    // @@@ last index of x in new array list is equal to specified index 
	    TestHelpers.AssertLastIndexOf(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListContainsX, index);

	    // @@@ contains x in old array list is true 
	    TestHelpers.AssertContains(Old_arrListContainsX, Old_x);

	    // @@@ contains x in new array list is true 
	    TestHelpers.AssertContains(New_arrListContainsX, x);
        }
    }
}
```

In this method, an array list object is created that is assumed to be under test by the PexRunner. We also specify that the contract is being tested by creating an assumption on an initial state of the array list object. 

- For some key properties:
  * `Count` of the array list has been preserved.
  * The previous value at the specified index has been replaced.
  * The new value at the specified index has been set.
 
After setting the value at the specified index:
 
- The properties `Count`, `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` are rechecked.
- If the `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` are working correctly and have the expected values, we can infer that the `Contains` property has also been correctly updated.
  
In the end, we'll do assertions that will be checked by Pex to validate that our assumptions are correct. If any of these assumptions are not met, the assertion will fail, indicating a problem in the test. 

This approach is an important takeaway from these kinds of tests because it uses the power of automatic test generation to verify the behavior of our system under different conditions. This becomes especially useful when writing complex code that may have logical issues.

For more specific examples of such tests please refer to my previous post on “Testing in the Real World” -- it provides code examples for the ArrayLists Class Contract Test in C#. Also, you can consider to follow the "Making Automated Unit Tests Work for You" by Roy Osherove, where the author gives a lot of insights about different areas of testing, including Unit tests in detail.

I hope this helped you, and if you have more questions, don't hesitate to ask.
