There's no "List.Add" method in the test case, but it is a common method to make your tests more useful and reduce the search space.
Also, you will need to handle the initialization of your ArrayList because a test case without the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute and the test case without the [PexMethod] attribute (this is the method where Pex starts) will cause these attributes in your list or any other variable they are modifying.
Hope this helps you make your tests more useful.
Note: Make sure you have the right reference for "AssumePrecondition" and "PexObserve". Make sure you add them to your using statement as well. Make sure you also have the NuGet package Microsoft.Pex installed.
Also remember to write all of your test cases inside a method with the [PexMethod], you won't be able to run the tests otherwise: 
public void YourTestMethod([PexAssumeUnderTest]TypeOfYourClass yourClassInstance)
Hope this helps you, and I hope that this post has helped you.
Remember to do thorough testing and do not test only the boundaries of your methods' input when your methods' functionality is well established.
Also, consider using Exceptions as a testing mechanism if the method is designed to throw an exception.
Remember that in addition to the tests using Pex, you are also required to write unit tests for your methods. Pex is not a replacement for unit testing.
There are also many other assertions you can write based on the functional requirements, like "the result should be greater than the input" or "the output should be in specific interval".
Add a method that can give initial condition (the list/array) of ArrayListTest and add [PexMethod] on it so that Pex can pick it up. For each test case in ArrayListTest, use [PexMethod].
Here's an example of the initial testing method:
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    [PexCustomAttribute("Initial Condition Method")]
    public void InitialArrayListTest(TestClass obj, string initialStr)
    {
        Assume.False(string.IsNullOrEmpty(initialStr));
        obj.AddTest(initialStr);
    }
}
Next, add an assert or an assume based on the contract of your method (Before and After the operation).
In the case of ArrayList.Add(), there are two basic operations that it performs: 
Adding an element to the end of the ArrayList.
Ensuring that the Count property has increased by 1.
Therefore, your testing method can be:
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    [PexCustomAttribute("Before Condition Test Case")]
    public void BeforeArrayListTest(TestClass obj, string initialStr)
    {
        Assume.False(string.IsNullOrEmpty(initialStr));
        int oldCount = obj.Count;
        obj.AddTest(initialStr);
        int newCount = obj.Count;
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(newCount == oldCount);
    }
}
Next, add a test case with [PexMethod] attribute.
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    [PexCustomAttribute("Before Condition Test Case")]
    public void BeforeArrayListTest(TestClass obj, string initialStr--------------------	    // it corresponds with the precondition of 'arrList.Contains(x)'
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
        }
    }
}


A: It appears you're trying to test the behavior of the ArrayList. You want to PUT values into specific indexes of an ArrayList and want to observe how the size of the ArrayList and its content change when you do so.
ArrayList is notorious for its poorly defined behavior when the index is out of range. What you're trying to achieve might be a misunderstanding, because Pex assumes that the index is valid when accessing/assigning a value.
A possible solution is to create a wrapper class that ensures the target index is valid before calling the appropriate ArrayList method.
```csharp
[PexClass]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    private class ArrayListWrapper
    {
        private ArrayList _list;

        public ArrayListWrapper(ArrayList list)
        {
            _list = list;
        }

        [PexCustomization("Insert")]
        public void Insert(int index, object value)
        {
            if (index < 0 || index > _list.Count)
                throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

            _list.Insert(index, value);
        }

        [PexCustomization("Set")]
        public void Set(int index, object value)
        {
            if (index < 0 || index >= _list.Count)
                throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

            _list[index] = value;
        }
    }

    // Test cases
}
 ```
With this wrapper, Pex should not generate test cases for invalid indexes. If you create test cases for this wrapper, Pex will automatically generate test cases for the underlying methods (`_list.Insert`, `_list[index] = value`) for valid indexes.
You might want to adjust the customization methods to match your exact requirements, or create more if you need to support more complex scenarios. 

This is an abstract solution. You might need to modify or refactor it based on your specific requirements and conditions. However, I hope this helps you to achieve your goal... 

Test for insert index should be like this:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestArrayListInsert()
{
    var wrapper = new ArrayListWrapper(new ArrayList());
    wrapper.Insert(/*Insert the invalid index here*/, new object());
}
```

Please change /*Insert the invalid index here*/ with invalid index such as -1 or more than count of arrayList. It should generate the assert or exception.

Test for set index should be like this:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestArrayListSet()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList {1,2,3};
    var wrapper = new ArrayListWrapper(arrList);
    wrapper.Set(/*Set the invalid index here*/, new object());
}
```

Again, Please change /*Set the invalid index here*/ with invalid index such as -1 or count of arrayList. It should generate the assert or exception.

Note: This example doesn't guarantee complete coverage for all corner cases. It's meant to illustrate how to approach the problem for the scenario you described.




















[PexClass] (or [PexClass(MaxExceptionTreeDepth)]), PexMethod, PexCustomization




Note: These attributes need a NuGet package called “Microsoft.Pex.Framework”. If the package isn't installed in Visual Studio, you need to install it.

Please note that you need to compile your classes with the "Just My Code" feature turned off. Just My Code in Visual Studio means that only your code is compiled for debugging session and not some other code that may be called by framework or libraries. This helps to avoid runtime issues related to third party libraries.

Please let me know if you need further assistance or have more questions.
I hope this helps you to achieve your goal... 

